bash for dietlibc
=================

This tree builds bash-4.2.53 with dietlibc, a small static C library,
producing a small static binary.  The dietlibc changes are the commits
on top of the 4.2.53 base.  They are guarded by __dietlibc__, or they are
needed only by dietlibc, so a glibc build is not affected.

Building
--------

    CC="diet gcc" ./configure --disable-nls --without-bash-malloc \
        --disable-help-builtin --disable-single-help-strings \
        --disable-disabled-builtins --disable-debugger \
        CFLAGS="-Os -ffunction-sections -fdata-sections -fno-asynchronous-unwind-tables -fno-stack-protector -fcf-protection=none -fno-pie" \
        LDFLAGS="-Wl,--gc-sections -no-pie"
    make
    strip bash

- --disable-nls is required.  Without it, lib/intl does not compile under
  dietlibc, because its argz.h and mempcpy() declarations conflict.  NLS
  could not work anyway: dietlibc's setlocale() always fails, so no message
  catalog is selected.
- --without-bash-malloc uses dietlibc's malloc, which is smaller.
- The Makefiles do not track config-bot.h as a dependency.  Run
  "make distclean" before rebuilding after changing it.
- -flto (in CFLAGS and LDFLAGS) saves about 10 KB of text section.  It
  builds and runs, but it has had less testing than the default flags.

Changes
-------

- config-bot.h defines errno as a macro under __dietlibc__.  dietlibc
  declares errno as thread-local, which conflicts with the plain
  "extern int errno;" that bash declares in many files.  The existing
  "#if !defined (errno)" guards then skip those declarations.
- lib/sh/oslib.c provides wctob(), which dietlibc lacks, and includes
  lib/sh/wcswidth.c.  configure leaves wcswidth.c out of the build when it
  finds a wcswidth(), so it is included here instead.
- lib/readline/terminal.c: dietlibc has no terminfo or termcap support, so
  tgetent() always fails.  readline then uses VT100/xterm sequences for
  cursor movement, clear-to-EOL and clear-screen, and binds the common
  ANSI/VT220 editing keys.  It also assumes autowrap, except for TERM=dumb.
  Without this, a wrapped line is followed by an explicit CR LF, and copying
  it from the terminal inserts extra newlines.
- lib/readline/my_wcwidth.c and include/my_wcwidth.h provide wcwidth().  See
  "Limitations".
- lib/readline/nls.c: if setlocale() fails and LC_CTYPE or LANG names a
  UTF-8 locale, readline uses its UTF-8 setup.  Without this, readline treats
  the session as the C locale and turns bytes >= 0x80 into meta characters.
- locale.c does not warn when setlocale() fails, which it always does under
  dietlibc.
- config-bot.h undefines HAVE_WCSWIDTH, so bash's own wcswidth() is used.
  dietlibc's version gives the wrong width for double-width characters.
- lib/sh/shmatch.c works around two regex gaps.  dietlibc's regex does not
  implement POSIX character classes, so [[:alpha:]] never matches; the
  classes are rewritten to ASCII ranges before regcomp().  It also leaves
  re_nsub at 0, which hides every capture group from BASH_REMATCH, so the
  groups are counted in the pattern instead.
- getcwd: configure finds getcwd(0, 0) broken on dietlibc (it returns
  EINVAL), so it sets GETCWD_BROKEN.  That makes bash build its own
  getcwd() in lib/sh/getcwd.c, which finds the current directory by walking
  the tree and comparing inode numbers.  The walk fails on bind mounts,
  overlay filesystems and similar setups, where the kernel can still name
  the directory, and it can fail with errno unset.  Under __dietlibc__,
  config-bot.h clears GETCWD_BROKEN, and get_working_directory() allocates the
  buffer for dietlibc's getcwd(), which calls the kernel.  glibc is not
  affected.
- lib/sh/eaccess.c and lib/termcap/tparam.c include unistd.h.  eaccess.c also
  declares faccessat(), which dietlibc prototypes only under _ATFILE_SOURCE.

Differences from a glibc build
------------------------------

Measured against a glibc build of the same source.  Where the two differ, the
glibc build is the reference.

Matching behavior, for the cases tested: line wrapping and copy-paste,
cursor movement, deletion and emoji width (within the wcwidth limits below),
UTF-8 input, and word motion over ASCII text.  The bash test suite gives the
same results as a glibc build for most sections (see "Test results").

Known differences:

- Word motion (M-f, M-b) over accented letters can differ.  dietlibc's
  iswalnum() treats non-ASCII letters as non-alphabetic, so "M-f" over
  "ñandú" stops inside the word.
- Case conversion of non-ASCII text is missing.  towupper() and towlower()
  are stubs.
- printf %q and other quoting of non-ASCII characters escape them as
  $'...' sequences.  glibc leaves printable UTF-8 characters unquoted.
- Regular expressions are byte-oriented.  In [[ ... =~ ... ]], "." matches
  one byte, so an emoji does not match "^.$".  POSIX character classes are
  ASCII-only.
- printf %e and %E print nothing.  dietlibc's printf does not implement
  them, even for a plain double.  %f and %g work.
- Time zones: dietlibc ignores zone names from the zoneinfo database.
  TZ=America/New_York gives GMT where glibc gives EDT.
- The text of some strerror() messages differs (for example, "Bad file
  descriptor" where glibc says "Bad file number").
- The locale is not selected by setlocale().  The only locale effect is
  UTF-8 detection from LC_CTYPE, LC_ALL or LANG, described above.
- Messages are not translated (see --disable-nls).

Limitations of the wcwidth() replacement
----------------------------------------

lib/readline/my_wcwidth.c holds compact tables of zero-width and
double-width code point ranges.  The default build covers emoji, which are
double-width, and the zero-width characters that emoji text uses: variation
selectors, zero-width joiner and space, skin-tone modifiers, and common
combining marks.  Two options add more ranges at compile time:

- MY_WCWIDTH_CJK covers CJK, Hangul and fullwidth forms, and CJK extensions
  in planes 2 and 3.  Without it, CJK characters count as one column each,
  so the cursor drifts after them.
- MY_WCWIDTH_ARABIC_HEBREW_THAI adds zero-width combining marks for those
  scripts.

Compared with the Unicode emoji data (the emoji package's fully-qualified
Emoji_Presentation entries), the default table is approximate.  About 250
code points in the emoji blocks are double-width here although they are
text-presentation by default (for example U+1F321 and U+1F336).  About 40
emoji are missing (for example U+1F004 and U+1F18E).  Exact tables would add a
few hundred bytes of text; they are not included.

Test results
------------

The bash test suite (tests/run-all) was run on a glibc build and on the
recommended dietlibc build, and the diffs for each section were compared.
The test machine did not have the en_US.UTF-8 locale that some tests use.

- Same results as glibc: assoc, coproc, execscript, read, test, trap, and
  cond (after the regex fix).
- builtins: the glibc build fails only because it prints a locale warning
  that the test does not expect.  The dietlibc build is silent and passes.
- intl: with the locale missing, glibc falls back to the C locale and gets
  the UTF-8 lines wrong.  dietlibc takes UTF-8 from the locale name, so those
  lines match.  Its remaining failures are decimal separators, which need
  locale data dietlibc does not have, and some Cyrillic string handling.  The
  cause of the Cyrillic difference was not investigated.
- printf: the %e and %E lines (see above), and the time zone lines.
- redir and vredir: strerror() wording only.

Measured sizes (text section, from size(1)), dietlibc: default flags 872,910;
-Os 677,211; -Os with section gc and no bash malloc 551,606; the recommended
build above 552,262 (599,496 bytes stripped).  glibc, dynamic, default
options: 1,013,005.  Resident memory of a shell after "true; pmap -x $$":
glibc 3368 KB RSS (244 KB dirty), dietlibc default build 832 KB (92 KB dirty).
